#: source/store/building.md:1
msgid "Building"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:3
msgid "As discussed in the [main page on derivations](./derivation/index.md):"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:5
msgid ""
"A derivation is a specification for running an executable on precisely "
"defined input to produce one or more [store objects](./store-object.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:7
msgid ""
"This page describes _building_ a derivation, which is to say following the "
"instructions in the derivation to actually run the executable. Some elements "
"of derivations are self-explanatory. For example, the arguments specified in "
"the derivation really are the arguments passed to the executable. In other "
"cases, however, there is additional common steps performed by Nix for all "
"derivations --- mostly for setting up the build environment and collecting "
"the built outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:12
msgid ""
"The chief design consideration for the building process is _determinism_. "
"Conventional operating systems are typically not designed with determinism "
"in mind. But determinism is needed to make Nix's build caching a transparent "
"abstraction."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:16 source/store/building.md:46
msgid "**Explanation**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:18
msgid ""
"For example, no one wants to slightly modify a derivation, and then find "
"that it no longer builds for an unrelated reason, because the original "
"derivation _also_ doesn't build anymore, but the cache hit on the original "
"derivation was hiding this. We want builds that succeed once to continue "
"succeeding, to encourage fearless modification of old build recipes. "
"Determinism is what enables things that once worked to keep working."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:22
msgid "The life cycle of a build can be broken down into 3 parts:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:24
msgid ""
"Spawn the builder process with the proper environment, including the correct "
"process arguments, environment variables, and file system state."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:26
msgid ""
"Wait for the builder process to exit and collect its exit status. Exit code "
"0 means success; anything else is a build failure. (Strictly speaking, Nix "
"detects process exit by waiting for the standard output and error streams to "
"close. If a builder explicitly closes these streams without exiting, Nix "
"will kill it, and deem the build a failure. Processes should therefore exit "
"_without_ explicitly closing those standard streams, and let the exiting of "
"the process close them implicitly.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:32
msgid ""
"Nix also logs the standard output and error of the process, but this is just "
"for human convenience and does not influence the behavior of the system. "
"(Builder processes have no idea what the consumer of their standard output "
"and error does with the pseudo-terminal master, only that they are indeed "
"consumed so buffers do not fill up etc. and writes to each output standard "
"stream will continue to succeed. In practice, Nix will store the log in `/"
"nix/var/log/nix`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:36
msgid "Processing the outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:38
msgid ""
"Traditionally, this happens only after the builder has exited: the builder "
"process should have left behind files for each output the derivation is "
"supposed to produce, and those files are processed to turn them into bona "
"fide store objects. Alternatively, the builder may send messages to Nix "
"while it's running, creating filesystem objects and linking them to an "
"output name. This allows outputs to be processed concurrently during the "
"build, allows outputs to depend on other newly created store objects, and "
"also resolves some tricky issues with content-addressing and output-to-"
"output references. If the processing succeeds, the resulting store objects "
"are associated with the derivation as (the results of) a successful build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:43
msgid ""
"Step (3) is done by Nix, either externally to the build (in the traditional "
"case, operating on the inert data left behind after the builder has exited "
"or been killed) or concurrently with it (in the IPC case). Step (1) however "
"is best described not from Nix's perspective, but from the build process's "
"perspective."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:48
msgid ""
"Ultimately, what matters for determinism is what the build process can "
"observe: what resources (files, networking, etc.) it can see, what syscalls "
"succeed or fail, etc. Nix can achieve this through many different sandboxing "
"strategies (namespaces, VMs, chroots, ...), but the process shouldn't be "
"able to tell them apart. We therefore specify building from the process's "
"perspective, not Nix's perspective, to focus on _what_, not _how_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:52
msgid "What derivations can be built"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:54
msgid ""
"Actually only some derivations are ready to be built. In particular, only "
"[_resolved_](./resolution.md) derivations can be built. That is to say, a "
"derivation that depends on other derivations is not ready yet to be built, "
"because some of those other derivations might not have yet been built. If "
"the other derivations are indeed all built, we can witness this fact by "
"resolving the derivation, and converting all the derivation's input "
"references into plain store paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:59 source/store/building.md:87
#: source/store/building.md:102 source/store/building.md:138
msgid "**Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:61
msgid ""
"Note that [input-addressing](derivation/outputs/input-address.md) "
"derivations are improperly resolved. As discussed on the linked page, the "
"current input-addressing algorithm does not respect resolution-equivalence "
"of derivations (\\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\)). That means that if Nix "
"properly resolved an input-addressed derivation, the resolved derivation "
"would have different input addresses, violating expectations. Nix therefore "
"improperly resolves the derivation, keeping its original input-addressed "
"output paths, creating an invalid derivation that is both resolved and "
"instructed to create the outputs at the originally expected paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:66
msgid "Environment of the builder process"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:68
msgid ""
"This section describes how the [`builder`](./derivation/index.md#builder) is "
"executed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:70 source/store/building.md:221
#: source/store/building.md:231
msgid "**Implementation detail**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:72
msgid ""
"Nix prevents multiple [Nix instances](../glossary.md#gloss-nix-instance) "
"from performing the same build at the same time, for example by acquiring "
"exclusive file locks."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:74
msgid "File system"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:76
msgid ""
"The builder should have access to a limited file system where only certain "
"objects are available. The most important exposed files are the inputs "
"(other store objects) of the (resolved) derivation. Additionally, some other "
"files are exposed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:80
msgid "Store inputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:82
msgid ""
"The builder will be run against a file system in which the [store directory]"
"(./store-path.md#store-directory-path) contains the [closure](../glossary."
"md#gloss-closure) of the inputs. In particular, consider a store that just "
"contains this closure. That store is exposed to the file system according to "
"the rules specified in the [Exposing Store Objects in OS File Systems](./"
"store-path.md#exposing) documentation. This precisely defines the file "
"system layout of the store that should be visible to the builder process."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:89
msgid ""
"Historically, Nix exposed _at least_ the following store contents to the "
"builder, but also arbitrarily other store objects, due to limitations around "
"operating systems' file system virtualization capabilities, and wanting to "
"avoid copying or moving files. It still can do this in so-called "
"_unsandboxed_ builds."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:92
msgid ""
"Such builds should be considered discouraged, but one that works less badly "
"against non-mischievous derivations than might be expected. This is because "
"store paths are relatively unpredictable, so a well-behaved program is "
"unlikely to stumble upon a store object it wasn't supposed to know about."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:95
msgid ""
"As operating systems developed better file system primitives, the need for "
"disabling sandboxing has lessened greatly over the years, and this trend "
"should continue into the future."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:97
msgid ""
"The outputs are expected to be created in that store directory as if they "
"were valid store objects. (They are just files during builder execution, but "
"during [processing outputs](#processing-outputs) they will be turned into "
"proper store objects.) The [environment variables](#env-vars) for each "
"output indicate where the builder should write them; Nix ensures that those "
"paths do not yet exist when the builder is run."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:104
msgid ""
"In sandboxed builds, ensuring that the outputs do not exist in the store "
"directory is trivial. In unsandboxed builds, it is harder in general. In the "
"worst case, the derivation is in fact rewritten so different output paths "
"are used instead, and then the outputs are rewritten back to the intended "
"output paths after. In the content-addressing case rewriting would be needed "
"either way, but in the input-addressing case, this is a significant "
"degradation, as the point of input addressing is to avoid rewrites by "
"knowing output paths in advance."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:113
msgid "Other file system state"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:115
msgid ""
"The current working directory of the builder process will be a fresh "
"temporary directory. It is initially empty when the process starts except "
"for a few input files:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:118
msgid ""
"If [`__structuredAttrs`](../language/advanced-attributes.md#adv-attr-"
"structuredAttrs) is enabled: `.attrs.json` (the derivation attributes as "
"JSON) and `.attrs.sh` (a Bash-compatible rendering of the same). The "
"environment variables `NIX_ATTRS_JSON_FILE` and `NIX_ATTRS_SH_FILE` point to "
"these files, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:121
msgid ""
"If [`passAsFile`](../language/advanced-attributes.md#adv-attr-passAsFile) is "
"used (only without `__structuredAttrs`): for each attribute name listed, a "
"file `.attr-<hash>` where `<hash>` is the [Nix32](../protocols/nix32.md)\\-"
"encoded SHA-256 hash of the attribute name. The environment variable "
"`<name>Path` points to the file containing the attribute's value."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:124
msgid ""
"In sandboxed builds, this directory is at a deterministic path inside the "
"sandbox (controlled by the [`sandbox-build-dir`](../command-ref/conf-file."
"md#conf-sandbox-build-dir) setting, default `/build`). See also the per-"
"store [`build-dir`](../store/types/local-store.md#store-local-store-build-"
"dir) setting for the host-side location."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:127
msgid ""
"Basic device nodes for essential operations (null device, random number "
"generation, standard streams as a pseudo terminal)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:129
msgid ""
"(A pseudo terminal would not be strictly necessary since the standard "
"streams are passively logging, not there to facilitate interaction. But it "
"is still useful to entice programs to do nicer logging with e.g. colors etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:132
msgid "On Linux: Process information via `/proc`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:134
msgid "Minimal user and group identity information"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:136
msgid "A loopback-only network configuration with hostname set to `localhost`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:140
msgid ""
"Fixed-output derivations have access to additional operating system state to "
"facilitate communication with the outside world, such as network name "
"resolution and TLS certificate verification. This is necessary because these "
"derivations are allowed to access the network, unlike regular derivations "
"which are fully sandboxed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:143
msgid "Environment variables"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:145
msgid ""
"The environment is cleared and set to the derivation attributes, as "
"specified above."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:148
msgid "For most derivations types this must contain at least:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:150
msgid ""
"For each output declared in `outputs`, the corresponding environment "
"variable is set to point to the intended path in the Nix store for that "
"output. Each output path is a concatenation of the cryptographic hash of all "
"build inputs, the `name` attribute and the output name. (The output name is "
"omitted if it's `out`.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:154
msgid "In addition, the following variables are set:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:156
msgid ""
"`NIX_BUILD_TOP` contains the path of the temporary directory for this build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:158
msgid ""
"Also, `TMPDIR`, `TEMPDIR`, `TMP`, `TEMP` are set to point to the temporary "
"directory. This is to prevent the builder from accidentally writing "
"temporary files anywhere else. Doing so might cause interference by other "
"processes."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:162
msgid ""
"`PATH` is set to `/path-not-set` to prevent shells from initialising it to "
"their built-in default value."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:164
msgid ""
"`HOME` is set to `/homeless-shelter`. (Without sandboxing, this discourages "
"programs from using `/etc/passwd` or the like to find the user's home "
"directory, which could cause impurity.) Usually, when `HOME` is set, it is "
"used as the location of the home directory, even if it points to a non-"
"existent path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:168
msgid ""
"`NIX_STORE` is set to the path of the top-level Nix [store directory path](./"
"store-path.md#store-directory-path) (typically, `/nix/store`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:170
msgid ""
"`NIX_ATTRS_JSON_FILE` & `NIX_ATTRS_SH_FILE` if `__structuredAttrs` is set to "
"`true` for the derivation. A detailed explanation of this behavior can be "
"found in the [section about structured attrs](../language/advanced-"
"attributes.md#adv-attr-structuredAttrs)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:173
msgid "Arguments"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:175
msgid ""
"The builder is passed the arguments specified by the derivation attribute "
"`args`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:177
msgid "Processing outputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:179
msgid ""
"There are two methods for processing outputs. But first, let us cover the "
"requirements common to both methods."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:182
msgid ""
"Regardless of which method is used, each output must be turned into a valid "
"store object. This involves two steps:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:185
msgid "**Normalize the file permissions**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:187
msgid ""
"The files must conform to the model described in the [Exposing in OS file "
"systems](./file-system-object/os-file-system.md) section. For example, "
"timestamps and permissions are canonicalised."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:190
msgid "\\[**Calculate the references**\\]{#scanning-for-references}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:192
msgid ""
"Nix scans each output path for [references](./store-object.md#references) to "
"input store objects by looking for the [digest](./store-path.md#digest) of "
"each input. (The name part and the [store directory path](./store-path."
"md#store-directory-path) are ignored when scanning; an input's hash part "
"that is neither followed by a `-` nor proceeded by a `/` still scans as a "
"reference.) Since these are potential runtime dependencies, Nix will "
"register them as references of the output store object they occur in."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:196
msgid ""
"This scanning can be disabled per output with the [_unsafe discard "
"references_](derivation/outputs/index.md#unsafe-discard-references) setting."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:198
msgid "Traditional (post-build) processing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:200
msgid ""
"With the traditional method, the builder process on exit should have left "
"behind files for each output the derivation is supposed to produce. The "
"files must be processed to turn them into bona fide store objects. If the "
"processing succeeds, those store objects are associated with the derivation "
"as (the results of) a successful build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:204
msgid ""
"Nix also scans for references from one output to another in the same way, "
"because outputs are allowed to refer to each other. The outputs' references "
"must form a [directed acyclic graph](../glossary.md#gloss-directed-acyclic-"
"graph). (This is not a special restriction for outputs; it is true for the "
"references of all store objects in general.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:208
msgid ""
"In the case of derivations with output paths that are fixed in advance (i.e. "
"[input-addressing](./derivation/outputs/input-address.md) derivations, or "
"[fixed content-addressing](./derivation/outputs/content-address.md#fixed) "
"derivations), the actual final store path to each output is used during the "
"build if possible. For [floating content-addressing](./derivation/outputs/"
"content-address.md#floating) derivations, however, the final store path is "
"not known in advance by definition. Scratch store paths must therefore be "
"used instead. Scanning will use those scratch paths, but then any output-to-"
"be that contains such a scanned scratch path must be rewritten to instead "
"use the final (content-addressed) path of the output in question."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:213
msgid ""
"In addition to output-to-output references, rewriting is also needed to "
"support self-references in the content-addressing case. An output may "
"contain its own store path digest, which is a self-reference. Hash functions "
"which are secure cannot allow the easy calculation of the quasi-fixed points "
"needed to support self-references \"natively\", so instead we replace all "
"would-be self-references with a sentinel value, and then rewrite the "
"sentinel value to be the final store path digest. Superficially, this post-"
"hashing rewriting breaks the content address, but as the self-references are "
"easily identified, the rewriting can be inverted to yield the original "
"hashed data, allowing verifying the content address after all."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:218
msgid ""
"At this point, the file system data is in the proper form, and the valid "
"acyclic reference data for each output is also calculated, so the outputs "
"are added to the store as proper store objects. Additionally, those store "
"objects (at least in the case that they are [content-addressed](./derivation/"
"outputs/content-address.md)) can be associated with the derivation in the "
"[build trace](./build-trace.md) in the record for a successful build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:223
msgid ""
"Nix will normally clean up and remove the temporary build directory after "
"every build, successful or unsuccessful. The builder doesn't know whether "
"Nix does or not, however, as it will have exited before the build directory "
"is cleaned up, and it will not see any old build directory if (after a "
"failed build) it is run again. The [`--keep-failed`](../command-ref/opt-"
"common.md#opt-keep-failed) option can be specified to keep the build "
"directory in the case of a failing build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:227
msgid "Concurrent processing via IPC"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:229
msgid ""
"With this method, the builder communicates with Nix during the build using "
"inter-process communication (IPC)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:233
msgid ""
"The current implementation, `builder-rpc-v0`, exposes its interface over a "
"limited form of the Nix daemon socket. Builders may use it either with their "
"own implementation of the Nix protocol, or with the `nix store add` and `nix "
"store submit-output` commands."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:236
msgid ""
"Derivations with `builder-rpc-v0` in their set of [`requiredSystemFeatures`]"
"(../language/advanced-attributes.md#adv-attr-requiredSystemFeatures) will "
"not receive output paths in their environment, and are expected to submit "
"all outputs with the aforementioned commands or protocol."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:239
msgid ""
"Instead of leaving files behind for Nix to process after exit, the builder "
"explicitly requests the daemon create store objects one at a time, then "
"sends commands assigning output names to the just-created store objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:241
msgid ""
"Scanning for references proceeds as usual for each store object creation "
"request, but the set of potential references to be scanned is greater: it "
"includes both all inputs (as before) and also all previously-added store "
"objects. This means, if output `bar` is supposed to reference output `foo`, "
"`foo` should be created first, and `bar` second."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:244
msgid ""
"All store objects being created are content-addressed (there is no support "
"for input-addressed outputs with the IPC approach). When a store object is "
"created, its content address store path will be calculated by Nix and then "
"returned in the IPC response message. The builder then knows what store path "
"to use in subsequent store objects in order for reference scanning to pick "
"them up."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:248
msgid "This overall approach has several advantages:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:250
msgid "**No Nix-side rewriting**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:252
msgid ""
"For content-addressed outputs, the builder is responsible for adding outputs "
"in reference order, using the store paths from earlier adds in later ones. "
"This avoids the fragile rewriting that would otherwise be needed to fix up "
"output-to-output references described above. The builder, unlike Nix itself, "
"is free to leverage domain-specific knowledge to do a better job. For "
"example it can"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:256
msgid ""
"uncompress, rewrite, and then recompress man pages, to not miss references "
"hidden by compression."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:258
msgid ""
"make sure to rewrite data that is to be signed, like Apple binaries, before "
"signing that data, so as not to invalidate any signatures by mistake."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:260
msgid "**Pipelining**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:262
msgid ""
"Downstream builds that only need some outputs (e.g., a \"dev\" or "
"\"headers\" output) can start without waiting for all outputs to be ready. "
"Nix doesn't yet implement this, but it could and should."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:265
msgid ""
"The major _disadvantage_ of this approach is that it doesn't yet support "
"self-references. Unlike acyclic output-to-output references, self-references "
"fundamentally do require rewriting. The output-to-output case was only a "
"challenge in the traditional case because all the outputs were submitted "
"simultaneously, whereas the self-reference case is fundamentally challenging "
"because of what it means for a hash function to be secure, as described "
"above. Neither batched (traditional) nor serial (IPC) submission of outputs "
"can avoid this fundamental property of secure hash functions. We could add "
"support for such rewriting just for self-references, as is done for the "
"traditional post-build processing, but we haven't yet done so as the very "
"point of the IPC approach is to free Nix from any obligation to rewrite "
"black-box data in unsound ways."
msgstr ""
